      body: 'When Grandpa Lonnie moved in with them in Louisville, the last box out of the truck was full of sermon CDs, labeled in his square handwriting. Fifteen-year-old Malik carried it upstairs and almost dropped it on the landing. "Granddad, nobody plays CDs anymore." Grandpa Lonnie laughed. "That’s why you’re going to help me put them on your phone." So on Saturday mornings they sat at the kitchen table with an old laptop, ripping one disc at a time. Some were choir anniversaries. Most were preachers. One was a man who talked like the barbershop and preached like his house was on fire. Malik found himself listening to the whole thing while the files copied. "He said our fight ain’t with people," Malik reported. "He said once you start focusing on the person, you lost." "Did he give you a verse?" Grandpa asked. Malik checked his notes. "Ephesians six." "Then look it up. Don’t take it from him or from me. Look it up." Malik read it aloud, and it said exactly what the preacher said. Then the next disc said something that bothered him, about a whole group of people controlling everything. Malik frowned. "Granddad, is that true?" Grandpa Lonnie took off his glasses. "That’s why we keep the Bible open while we listen. A man can be right about Ephesians and wrong about people. You keep what the Word confirms, and you let the Word correct the rest. That ain’t disrespect. That’s what the Bereans did." Malik started a note on his phone with two columns, Holds and Corrected, and by the end of the summer it had a hundred lines. His grandfather asked him to read it at the family reunion. Malik read the Holds first. Then he read the verse at the top: "Prove all things; hold fast that which is good." (1 Thessalonians 5:21).',
