        body: 'Every Saturday Tasha and Jerome Whitfield cleaned the house in Gary together, and every Saturday they fought. It was always about something small. The towels. The way he loaded the dishwasher. The way she said, "I’ll just do it myself." This Saturday it was the trash. Jerome forgot to take it out, and Tasha slammed the lid and said, "You always do this. You never think about me." Jerome stood in the kitchen with a dish towel over his shoulder and, for the first time in eleven years, did not answer back. He had been listening to an old sermon in the car that week, a preacher saying that once you start focusing on the person, you have already lost the fight. He took a breath and said, "Baby, who forgot to take out the trash on you before me?" Tasha looked at him like he had lost his mind. Then she sat down at the kitchen table, and out it came: her father, who forgot everything, her birthday, her recital, the day he promised to pick her up from her grandmother’s house and never came. She had been nine, sitting on the porch with her bag packed until it got dark. "It’s not the trash," she said. "It’s that porch." Jerome sat down next to her. They did not finish cleaning. They read Ephesians 6:12 off his phone, and he prayed for the little girl on the porch, and she prayed for the man her father never was. Then they called her father, who was seventy-three now and lived alone in Indianapolis, and invited him to Sunday dinner. The trash went out that afternoon. Jerome took it. Tasha watched him from the window and laughed, the first time a Saturday had ended that way in a long time.',
