        title: 'The Group Chat',
        body: 'The Cousins group chat had forty-one members and one rule nobody had written down: never bring up church. Then Aunt Bernice shared a video of the preacher she watched every morning, and Cousin Darius, who had been studying hard for two years, replied with a link titled Ten Reasons This Man Is a False Teacher. Within an hour there were ninety messages. Scripture flew both ways like dodgeballs. Somebody brought up a pastor from 1998. Somebody’s husband left the chat. By ten that night Darius was typing a twelve-paragraph answer when his phone buzzed with a private message from his grandmother. "Baby, call me." He called. Mother Ruth was eighty-four and still taught the women’s class at Mount Olive in Durham. "Did you win?" she asked. Darius laughed. "Not yet." "How many souls got saved in that chat tonight?" He did not answer. "Darius, I watched that man you’re fighting about bring your aunt back to church after Uncle Leon died. She sat in her house two years and wouldn’t come out. He might be wrong about some things. I don’t follow everything he says. But I’m going to honor what the Lord used him to do for my daughter." Then she read him something from her big-print Bible, slowly. It was Paul, saying the servant of the Lord must not strive but be gentle unto all men. Darius deleted the twelve paragraphs. He wrote two sentences in the chat instead: "I’m sorry for how I said it, Aunt Bernice. Can I come to Sunday dinner and we can talk about it face to face?" She sent back a heart. The chat went quiet. On Sunday they sat at the table with two Bibles and a plate of greens, and nobody won, and everybody stayed.',
