        title: 'The Man Who Left Every Church',
        body: 'Brother Wendell Hayes could quote more Scripture than anybody at New Bethel in Kansas City, and he let everyone know it. He corrected the pastor after sermons. He corrected the Sunday school teacher in front of her class. When a visiting evangelist mispronounced a Hebrew word, Wendell posted a forty-minute video about it. When the elders asked to meet with him, he called them false shepherds and left. He did that at three churches in nine years. Eventually he stopped going anywhere. He had his own channel now, a ring light, and two thousand subscribers who agreed with him about everything. He called it a remnant. His wife Lorraine went to church alone. One winter Wendell had a stroke. Lorraine called the number on his channel’s page, looking for anyone from that remnant who might come pray. Nobody came. Nobody even knew his real name. The people who came were the elders from New Bethel, the ones he had called false shepherds. They sat in the hospital waiting room in shifts for three days. Pastor Odom brought Lorraine dinner and paid for her parking. When Wendell could speak again, slurred and slow, the first thing he said to Pastor Odom was, "Why are you here?" Pastor Odom said, "Because we know you. And you are ours." Wendell cried like a child. He spent the next year relearning how to walk, and relearning something else. He never did make another video. When he could stand, he stood up in a Wednesday night service at New Bethel and asked forgiveness of every person he had shamed, by name. Then he sat down beside Lorraine and stayed.',
