        title: 'The Thermos',
        body: 'Coach Antoine Brooks kept a thermos of coffee on the bench at every one of his daughter’s winter soccer games in Milwaukee. By the second half it was always cold. "How does that happen?" eight-year-old Imani asked one Saturday, wrapped in a blanket. "I closed it tight." "Nothing’s wrong with the thermos," her father said. "It just sat out in the cold too long. Whatever’s around it gets into it." Imani thought about that all the way home. That night, when her father checked on her, she was still awake. "Daddy, is that why you don’t like Uncle Ray?" He sat down slowly. Uncle Ray had borrowed money for his trucking business six years ago and never paid it back, and Antoine had not spoken to his brother since. He never said anything about it at home. He did not know Imani had noticed. "What do you mean, baby?" "You used to laugh at the cookout. Now you just sit by the grill. Mama says you got cold." He laughed in spite of himself. Then he did not laugh. He thought about who he had been spending his time with lately, the guys at the shop who complained about everybody, the videos he watched late at night about people who cheat you. He had surrounded himself with cold. The next week he did two things. He went back to the men’s Bible study he had quit. And on Sunday after church he drove to Uncle Ray’s house with Imani in the back seat and a fresh thermos of hot coffee. Ray opened the door looking scared. Antoine handed him the coffee. "I came to say I forgive you," he said. "And I’ve missed you." Imani told everybody at school on Monday that her daddy had warmed up.',
