        title: 'The Seat by the Window',
        body: 'Sister Gloria Henderson had sat by the same window at Zion Hill in Cleveland for forty years. She remembered when the old pastor passed over her husband for the deacon board in 1987. She remembered when the choir director gave her solo to a younger woman. She remembered every slight, and she told the new members about every one of them, kindly, at the repast, over pound cake. "Watch that pastor," she would say. "He’ll use you up." Young couples who came to Zion Hill rarely stayed past their first year. Nobody connected it to Sister Gloria. One Sunday the new associate pastor, a young woman named Reverend Keisha Tate, preached on Psalm 1, the blessed man who does not walk in the counsel of the ungodly, nor stand in the way of sinners, nor sit in the seat of the scornful. She said bitterness does not only spoil your own fruit; it reaches over and spoils other people’s. Sister Gloria felt the words go through her like a blade. She went home and did not eat. That night she counted, on the back of an envelope, the young families she had warned away. She stopped at eleven because she could not see through her tears. The next Sunday she asked Reverend Tate for a meeting. "I’ve been sitting in that seat a long time," she said. "I didn’t know it was the scornful’s seat." They prayed together. Then Sister Gloria did something nobody at Zion Hill had ever seen her do. She asked the church for forgiveness from the floor, and she wrote a letter to each of the eleven families. Four of them came back. She sat with one young couple every Sunday after that, not by the window but in the front, holding their baby during the sermon.',
