        title: 'Big Mama’s Thanksgiving',
        body: 'Every Thanksgiving, Big Mama Odessa Carter cooked for thirty people in her house in Shreveport, and every Thanksgiving, sometime between the macaroni and the sweet potato pie, she cried. Nobody helped her, she said. Nobody appreciated her. She had been up since four. Her grandchildren tiptoed around the kitchen afraid to touch anything, and her sons argued in the driveway about whose turn it was to feel guilty. This year her granddaughter Simone, home from her first semester at Grambling, did something new. She sat down next to Big Mama on the porch on Wednesday night and asked, "Big Mama, who asked you to cook for thirty?" Big Mama looked at her like she had cussed. "Nobody had to ask. That’s what I do." "I know," Simone said. "And we love you for it. But you’re seventy-six. What if this year everybody brought something, and you just made your dressing and sat down?" Big Mama was quiet a long time. Then she said, "If I sit down, what am I for?" Simone held her hand. "Big Mama, Jesus told Martha that Mary chose the good part, and Mary was just sitting with Him. You’re for us. Not for the cooking." Big Mama did not answer. But on Thursday there were eleven dishes on the table that she had not cooked, and one pan of dressing that she had. She sat in the good chair in the living room all afternoon, and the great-grandbabies climbed on her, and she told the story about the year the oven caught fire in 1971, and everybody laughed until they cried. Nobody cried any other way. That night she called Simone at her mother’s house. "Baby," she said, "I didn’t know I could be tired and happy at the same time."',
