        title: 'The Word No',
        body: 'For six years Andre Mitchell believed that if he could just love Kendra right, she would stop being hurt. He worked double shifts at the plant in Flint, paid every bill, came straight home, went to church with her every Sunday. And every week there was something. He had looked at a woman in the grocery store. He had not texted back fast enough. He had talked to his mother too long on the phone. If he defended himself, Kendra cried and said he was just like her father, who had walked out when she was seven. So Andre stopped defending himself. He stopped calling his mother. He stopped going to the men’s fellowship because Kendra said he loved those men more than her. One Sunday Pastor Hall preached on Martha, the woman who took on what nobody asked and then blamed her sister, and on how some people learn to control others with their pain. Afterward Andre sat in his truck in the parking lot and did not start it. He realized he had not said the word no in six years. That night, very gently, he said it. Kendra wanted him to skip his nephew’s graduation because she did not feel welcome there. "No," he said. "I love you. I’m going. I want you to come with me." She screamed for an hour. Then she did not speak to him for three days. On the fourth day she asked to see Pastor Hall and his wife. In their office she said, for the first time out loud, that she had been punishing Andre for something her father did. She said, "It’s me." It was not the end of their trouble. They went to counseling for two years. But it was the first night of the real marriage, and they both knew it.',
